t, version, enabled flag rendered as "true"/"false"); caller-supplied free text (display_name, schedule, hostname even — schedule is a cron-style expression but is still caller-controlled bytes) is NEVER recorded verbatim, because a customer pasting a token into one of those fields is exactly the footgun a defensive convention avoids. The httpapi-layer composition is the canonical create-handler shape — createXxxRequest decoder (Enabled / boolean-default-true fields are pointers to preserve the absent-vs-explicit-false distinction at the JSON seam — `*bool` decoded; an omitted field collapses to the default at the handler boundary, an explicit `false` rides through unchanged; integer fields take their schema-side default when zero) + createXxxPayload + createXxxHandler that gates on RequireAuth + the typed-internal nil-creator guard + validate.DecodeJSON (strict, max-body-bytes, unknown-field rejection, no input echo on parse failure) + creator.Create + http.StatusCreated yalla.output.v1 envelope projecting through serviceXxxOf. Action is CapWrite (ActionXxxCreate -> CapWrite in catalog.go): owner / admin / developer allowed by role; CI / viewer / support denied via ReasonDeniedNoCapability — load-bearing distinction: a CapDeploy sibling action (e.g. ActionBackupRun, ActionDeploymentCreate) WOULD admit CI. The wire route uses serviceIDResolver (no parent_project_id leg pinned on the resource scope), so EVERY project-/env-/service-scoped grant is denied at the boundary via the engine's one-way covers() rule (ReasonDeniedOutOfScope); only an organization-wide write role or an org-level write grant reaches the handler. Cross-tenant: the resolver pins resource scope to the principal's home org id (never the path service's tenant), so cross-tenant service_ids hit the persistence layer with the home org id and surface as a deterministic apierr.NotFound — engine cross-tenant deny is pinned at the engine level (defence-in-depth: EVERY role including Support is denied cross-